# Mutation case for "a hazard is published before it is checked".
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/a_hazard_is_published_before_it_is_checked.cases`.
#
# The LSCQ row of SCE Protocol-Synthesis RFC §synth-5-P frees a segment through
# hazard pointers (`queue::hazard`). A participant names the segment it is about
# to touch in its slot, and only then reads the location it validates against
# again; the scan that frees a retired segment reads the slots after the
# retirement. For the scan to see a participant that has validated, the
# publication has to come before the check in the one order every thread shares,
# and `Hazard::protect` gives that with two sequentially consistent accesses.
# The case below takes both to `Relaxed`, so a participant can validate a segment
# the scan has already read past and is about to free.
#
# The oracle is the queue's real-thread tests (`queue_threads.rs`) built with
# ThreadSanitizer, the build the forge-rust gate runs them under. It is not the
# loom models and not Miri, and the measurements that put it here are these
# (2026-10-10, the build machine):
#
#   - loom 0.7.2 reports a race in the UNMODIFIED handshake. A probe written
#     with plain loom atomics and `SeqCst` on both sides, a protocol that is
#     correct under C11, fails on its first execution: loom lets a `SeqCst` load
#     read a value the sequential order has already replaced. A model that fails
#     on correct code cannot judge a weakening of it.
#   - Miri, the gate's own command, eight seeds, passed all four LSCQ real-thread
#     tests with the publication weakened and reported nothing.
#   - ThreadSanitizer reported a data race on twenty of twenty runs of the four
#     LSCQ tests with it weakened, and on none of the unmodified tree. The race
#     it names is the consequence the hazard exists to prevent: a producer's
#     atomic read of a segment's `next` (`lscq.rs`, `try_push`) against another
#     thread's plain write of the same address as it builds a new segment, so a
#     segment was freed, its memory reused, and a participant still read it. The
#     process exits non-zero while the tests print `ok`, which is how this harness
#     already reads a sanitizer report: a binary that exits non-zero with no
#     failed test counted is red, and named.
#
# What that does and does not establish. The sanitizer sees the use-after-free,
# not the ordering that allowed it, and it sees it only on a schedule that
# produces one. Twenty of twenty on one machine says the schedule is common
# there; it is not a promise about a slower host, and a SURVIVED here would be a
# finding about the oracle's reach rather than permission to weaken the
# publication. The other half of the guarantee, that a scan keeps what a hazard
# names, is `a_scan_keeps_what_a_hazard_names.cases`, and its oracle is exact.
#
# The build is not cargo's default, so the casefile declares what it needs. The
# flags are the selector's. The toolchain is `mutation_env RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN`,
# read from `scripts/lib/queue_sanitizer.sh`, which is where the forge-rust gate
# reads it, so the two cannot name different nightlies. `-Zbuild-std` rebuilds
# the standard library with the sanitizer (its own target directory, so the flag
# does not invalidate the ordinary build), which is why the round needs the
# `rust-src` component, and why the mutation-rounds gate installs the toolchain
# for a casefile that declares one.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

source "$SCE_REPO_ROOT/scripts/lib/queue_sanitizer.sh"
mutation_env "RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN=$QUEUE_SANITIZER_NIGHTLY"
mutation_tests -Zbuild-std --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu --target-dir target/tsan --config 'build.rustflags=["-Zsanitizer=thread"]' -p sce-forge-runtime --test queue_threads
mutation_targets backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/queue/hazard.rs
mutation_oracles backends/rust/forge-runtime/tests/queue_threads.rs

mutation_case "a participant publishes its hazard without ordering it before the check" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/queue/hazard.rs",
     r'''            self.slot().store(pointer.cast(), Ordering::SeqCst);
            let again = source.load(Ordering::SeqCst);''',
     r'''            self.slot().store(pointer.cast(), Ordering::Relaxed);
            let again = source.load(Ordering::Relaxed);''')
PY
